    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 5,
        "sourceEnd": 146,
        "text": "Okay, let's start. Our task is to compare two learning theories: behaviorism and constructivism. Ben, you looked at behaviorism first, right?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "402da5f747630939b508df35a894ee3110e1970e804b740982551ae1880e552c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 152,
        "sourceEnd": 406,
        "text": "Yes. Behaviorism says learning is a change in behavior caused by stimuli and responses. For example, a teacher gives a gold star for correct answers, and students repeat the behavior. It focuses on observable actions, not on what happens inside the mind.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "8011974f8cdeb5320f298c4c703243b4b4cf535830fa2c0dbcf594344fc9f05e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 412,
        "sourceEnd": 435,
        "text": "So it ignores thinking?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "cdca80fde36533a019ffb58d81f3d783fe591d6e6775d167dbb8bbcb4ca3f138",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 441,
        "sourceEnd": 565,
        "text": "Not exactly. It just doesn't study mental processes. It works well for simple skills, like memorizing multiplication tables.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "e72a25365dc0d895fbd575e0544b3db147dc793a4870f37eab758ee2bd4d5e9a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 571,
        "sourceEnd": 617,
        "text": "Thanks. Now Clara, you studied constructivism.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "e3d2bc9f37958cfbe00d8e7821f4acd8940614941f7abe1fb135373e8794607e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "clara",
        "speaker": "Clara",
        "sourceStart": 625,
        "sourceEnd": 875,
        "text": "Right. Constructivism argues that learners build knowledge by connecting new information to what they already know. The teacher acts as a guide, not a lecturer. For instance, students might do a project on local history and discover facts themselves.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "bbd4961f35d576292bdb7a362e6b63d6d1167e672606964e2c93ac46ac6cecca",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 881,
        "sourceEnd": 944,
        "text": "But isn't that too slow? Some students need clear instructions.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "802927e0ffd460797cf2abdb15815359d29d20f4af5fd7493648dddf102841f7",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "clara",
        "speaker": "Clara",
        "sourceStart": 952,
        "sourceEnd": 1138,
        "text": "That's a fair point. However, constructivism doesn't reject instruction; it just emphasizes active learning. Although direct teaching is efficient, it may not develop deep understanding.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "f4f08c683dc54b376745b6e79a02cd03f7d7f3d74c012f200fe13b4ef636a17a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 1144,
        "sourceEnd": 1261,
        "text": "Let's note that. So behaviorism is teacher-centered, while constructivism is student-centered. What about assessment?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "67b9a86ace91a0d6926cfbff1e805fc29e357fba10acaf86102956dc0a177e4b",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 1267,
        "sourceEnd": 1363,
        "text": "Behaviorism uses tests with right or wrong answers. Constructivism uses portfolios and projects.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "15a57af6e252c8a6b6511389d3b2f7726e1fd180334603d82e7d984112751fc3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "clara",
        "speaker": "Clara",
        "sourceStart": 1371,
        "sourceEnd": 1506,
        "text": "Yes, and group work is common in constructivist classrooms. Even though group work can be noisy, it helps students explain their ideas.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "71f4225372c65573d4df3a3c8b639e49718539d723ad483bb194933e14157e95",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 1512,
        "sourceEnd": 1546,
        "text": "Good. Now, which theory is better?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "41d79d93b5f56bb126938f461eff1da051849970d23cbc56634457f154dc3c19",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 1552,
        "sourceEnd": 1704,
        "text": "I don't think one is better. It depends on the goal. For basic facts, behaviorism is practical. For complex problem-solving, constructivism is stronger.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "12801e9afde6b0ec84d94becdf9a43109769d46b125be9a5c60a93f90a9eb79d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "clara",
        "speaker": "Clara",
        "sourceStart": 1712,
        "sourceEnd": 1816,
        "text": "I agree. In fact, many teachers mix both. They give clear instructions first, then let students explore.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "ce9e5ca2064ea0fc22e537d2e63297fd1ef1f4b6d6759fe6121de7b1b22d7282",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 1822,
        "sourceEnd": 2086,
        "text": "That's a useful conclusion. Let's summarize: behaviorism focuses on observable behavior and reinforcement; constructivism focuses on building understanding through experience. Both have strengths. Our presentation will compare them and suggest blending approaches.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "faeff4a4fedd26b17b413cd67632cd4abfc24e225d2dea828dd0b5754233478c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "ben",
        "speaker": "Ben",
        "sourceStart": 2092,
        "sourceEnd": 2181,
        "text": "One more thing: the textbook we read is from 2018, so it doesn't include recent research.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "b6334535c44db5b579aab90b49c07ae7228c1a56b1107a1e4cb5ce60b79c49ca",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 2187,
        "sourceEnd": 2256,
        "text": "Noted. We'll mention that limitation. Okay, let's prepare our slides.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "6bdb4358c6876eef3c9c966cf2009f0e04ad302e40b819b35c035b0221ca7433",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
